#open-question(title: [Recognizing Mild and Lifetime-Energy ME/CFS — Separating the Tired Constitution from the Disease])[
Patients who have "always been tired" present a diagnostic problem: the shared "new or definite onset (not lifelong)" criterion across Fukuda, Canadian Consensus, and IOM excludes them by assumption @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue, yet lifelong fatigue is hard to operationalize, rarely met even by patients describing long-standing fatigue, and non-discriminating on symptoms and function @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue. Whether these mild/lifetime-energy patients are a distinct predisposing state, the mild end of the same illness, or a separate constitution remains unresolved.

*Proposed recognition approach.* Treat *baseline energy level* and *post-exertional malaise (PEM)* as two independent axes rather than a single "fatigue" question @Jason2010FatigueTypes @Brown2013EnergyEnvelope: (1) screen lifetime energy level and current PEM separately; (2) do not use "lifelong fatigue" as an exclusion @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue; (3) use PEM presence as the gate to a full ME/CFS assessment; (4) offer low-harm energy-envelope/pacing care to low-energy patients regardless of diagnosis (access). This is a recognition/access pathway, not a treatment protocol.

*Research questions.* (a) Would an empirical, pre-registered test of removing the lifelong-fatigue exclusion change who is diagnosed without diluting the syndrome? (b) Does a prospective cohort of lifetime-low-energy individuals tracking the onset of PEM show a mild→moderate/severe transition (the central evidence gap)? @Collin2015childFatigue

*Critical caveat (why this is not settled).* Broadening recognition risks over-diagnosing ME/CFS in tired-but-well people; PEM — not baseline energy — remains the discriminator, and the mild end may lack a detectable PEM signature @Harvey2009neurasthenia.

*(Severity applicability: mild / lifetime-energy. Moderate-to-severe transition unmeasured.)*

*Consequence:* If separating baseline energy from PEM improves recognition without over-broadening diagnosis, clinicians would stop dismissing the "always tired" patient and instead look specifically for a new, definite PEM component.

*(Origin: brainstorm.)*
] <oq:mild-lifetime-case-recognition>
